\description{
\code{g.bay()} takes the inputs of \code{\link[DescTools:GTest]{DescTools::GTest()}} and answers the opposite question: not
"how surprising are these counts if there is no link?", but "how probable is a link given
these counts?". It covers the same two cases:
\itemize{
\item \strong{goodness of fit} of one variable to given proportions \code{p} (equal by default);
\item \strong{independence} of two variables in an r x c contingency table.
}

The way is chosen alone, from the form of the input, and told step by step as \code{\link[=m.test]{m.test()}}
and \code{\link[=m.bay]{m.bay()}} do. The bilan ranks the two hypotheses (link or independence; departure or
fit) by posterior probability, with a plain qualifier and the Bayes factor, never a p-value,
then says where the link lies (which cells), how large it is, and gives a sentence ready for
a report.
}
\section{Method (dev/gbay/DECISIONS.md, G1 to G12)}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Goodness of fit}: multinomial counts; under "no departure" the proportions are exactly
\code{p}, under "departure" they follow a uniform Dirichlet. The Bayes factor has a closed form
(Good 1967; the model of \code{multibridge}, Sarafoglou et al. 2023).
\item \strong{Independence}: Dirichlet-multinomial with neutral margins (closed form). Under a link,
each row has its own proportions, Dirichlet(a); under independence, the rows share their
proportions, Dirichlet(r a), which is the law a link gives to the margin. Balanced margins
thus say nothing of a link, and the Bayes factor is the same whether the total, the rows
or the columns were fixed. It is the BDe score of Bayesian networks (Heckerman et al.
1995) and the Gunel and Dickey (1974) Bayes factor with coherent margins. a = 2, chosen in
advance: under a link, the prior median of Cramer's V is 0.2 to 0.4, a medium effect.
Measured on 1 000 tables per scenario: under independence, a link announced at P > 0.95 in
0.7 \% of the tables at most (1.2 \% with very unequal margins); under a moderate link (V = 0.2), independence strongly
supported in up to 7 \% of the bilans (dev/gbay/VALIDATION.md, sections 10 and 12). Two
other engines were measured and set aside: the default Bayes factors of
\code{\link[BayesFactor:contingencyTableBF]{BayesFactor::contingencyTableBF()}} (up to 90 \% of false strong links in 4 x 5 tables)
and the BIC of the log-linear model (strong support for independence in up to 89 \% of
tables with a moderate link).
\item \strong{Coherence}: if independence (or the fit) leads with strong support while some cells
depart from it with a 95 \% interval that excludes 1, its support is brought back to
moderate and the bilan says why.
\item \strong{2 x 2 tables}: odds ratio with its 95 \% credible interval.
\item \strong{Cells}: for each cell, how many times it is more (or less) frequent than without a
link, with a 95 \% credible interval: the Bayesian counterpart of the standardised
residuals. A cell is read when its interval excludes 1.
\item \strong{Size}: Cramer's V a posteriori (Cohen's w for a goodness of fit). It is positive even
without a link: a size, not a proof.
\item \strong{Small counts}: no minimum count and no correction (exact computation); small counts
make the answer less decisive.
\item \strong{Power}: for a moderate link (V = 0.2) at N = 200, 60 to 74 \% of the tables give
P > 0.75, against 81 to 90 \% for the G test at p < 0.05; for a goodness of fit, clearly
lower than the G test, in exchange for almost no false "yes".
\item \strong{Sensitivity}: the concentration of the Dirichlet (1, 2, 4 for independence; 1/2, 1, 2
for a goodness of fit), crossed with the starting chances (1 against 2 to 2 against 1).
}

Not handled: tables with three dimensions or more; paired data (McNemar); which groups
differ two by two (use \code{\link[=m.bay]{m.bay()}} with \code{y ~ g}).
}

\examples{
# Goodness of fit: 3:1 Mendelian ratio (the counts of GTest's help page).
g.bay(c(705, 224), p = c(3, 1) / 4, verbose = FALSE)$prob_effect

# Independence in a 2 x 3 table (a table of counts).
tab <- matrix(c(20, 15, 25, 30, 10, 12), nrow = 2,
              dimnames = list(sex = c("F", "M"), colour = c("blue", "green", "red")))
r <- g.bay(tab)
r$cells[, c("row", "col", "ratio", "ratio_low", "ratio_high")]

# 2 x 2: odds ratio with its credible interval.
g.bay(matrix(c(18, 7, 9, 16), 2), verbose = FALSE)$odds_ratio

# Two factors in a data.frame, or a response and a factor.
d <- data.frame(treat = rep(c("cold", "warm"), each = 30))
set.seed(1)
d$germ <- ifelse(runif(60) < ifelse(d$treat == "warm", 0.8, 0.5), "yes", "no")
g.bay(germ ~ treat, data = d, verbose = FALSE)$prob_effect
g.bay(d$treat, d$germ, verbose = FALSE)$prob_effect

# A data.frame of counts, one row per combination.
k <- data.frame(eye = c("blue", "blue", "brown", "brown"), hair = c("fair", "dark", "fair", "dark"),
                n = c(32, 11, 10, 41))
g.bay(n ~ eye + hair, data = k, verbose = FALSE)$prob_effect

}
